<article xml:id="session3wks" label="3401">

  <title>MATH 142 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <worksheet>

    <title>Session #4 (2.3, 2.4)</title>

    <paragraphs>

  <title>Getting Warmed Up and In the Right Mindset</title>

  <p>
    Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you. 
        For additional practice problems and explanations, refer to the Math 142 textbook which is free and can be downloaded as a PDF 
        at <url href="https://oaktrust.library.tamu.edu/items/03980291-5e85-4d2e-a742-961ac49666aa"> Texas A&amp;M The OAKTrust Digital Repository </url>.
  </p>

  <exercise workspace="2in" number="1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The introductory derivative rules are given below. For each rule, fill in the blank.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of a Constant:</em>
            For any real number <m>k</m>, <m>\frac{d}{dx}(k)=</m>
            <fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of a Power Function:</em>
            For any real number <m>n</m>,
            <m>\frac{d}{dx}(x^n)=</m><fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of an Exponential Function:</em>
            For any real number <m>b&gt;0</m>,
            <m>\frac{d}{dx}(b^x)=</m><fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of a Logarithmic Function:</em>
            For any real number <m>b&gt;0</m>,
            <m>\frac{d}{dx}(\log_b(x))=</m><fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of the Sum of Two Differentiable Functions:</em>
            <m>\frac{d}{dx}(f(x)+g(x))=</m><fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of the Difference of Two Differentiable Functions:</em>
            <m>\frac{d}{dx}(f(x)-g(x))=</m><fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Derivative of a Constant Multiple of a Differentiable Function:</em>
            For any real number <m>k</m>,
            <m>\frac{d}{dx}(k\cdot f(x))=</m><fillin characters="15"/>
          </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in" number="2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be two differentiable functions.
        Circle the correct <em>Product Rule</em> for derivatives.
      </p>

      <ol marker="1.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>
              \frac{d}{dx}(f(x)\cdot g(x))
              =
              \left(\frac{d}{dx}f(x)\right)\cdot
              \left(\frac{d}{dx}g(x)\right)
            </m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>
              \frac{d}{dx}(f(x)\cdot g(x))
              =
              f(x)\cdot\left(\frac{d}{dx}g(x)\right)
              +
              g(x)\cdot\left(\frac{d}{dx}f(x)\right)
            </m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>
              \frac{d}{dx}(f(x)\cdot g(x))
              =
              f(x)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx}g(x)\right)\cdot
              g(x)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx}f(x)\right)
            </m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in" number="3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be two differentiable functions.
        Circle the correct <em>Quotient Rule</em> for derivatives.
      </p>

      <ol marker="1.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>
              \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right)
              =
              \frac{\frac{d}{dx}(f(x))}
                   {\frac{d}{dx}(g(x))}
            </m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>
              \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right)
              =
              \frac{
                g(x)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx}f(x)\right)
                -
                f(x)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx}g(x)\right)
              }{g(x)}
            </m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>
              \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right)
              =
              \frac{
                g(x)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx}f(x)\right)
                -
                f(x)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx}g(x)\right)
              }{(g(x))^2}
            </m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Let us get our hands on!</title>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="1">
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Find <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> for each of the following. It would help to refer to the derivative rules from the Warm up.
    </p>
 </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
        <p><m>y = 3x^2</m></p>
    </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p><m>y = 27x + 5</m></p>
     </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p><m>y = e^{-5}</m></p>
     </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p><m>y = 8^x</m></p>
     </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p><m>y = \log_2(x) + 2e^x</m></p>
     </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <p><m>y = \ln(x) + 2x^{-5}</m></p>
     </task>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="2">

  <statement>
    <p>
      Given <m>f(x)=2x^3+3e^x-5x^{-2}</m> find <m>f'(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="3">

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find
      <m>
        \frac{d}{dt}
        \left(
        \sqrt[3]{t^2}
        +5\log_2(t)
        -\frac{7}{t^{4/3}}
        \right)
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="4">

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find
      <m>
        \frac{d}{dx}
        \left(
        \frac{4}{x^5}
        +4^x
        \right)
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise workspace="1in" number="5">

  <statement>
    <p>
      Find
      <m>
        \frac{d}{dx}
        \left(
        \frac{5x^4-6x^{-3}+9}{x^2}
        \right)
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="6">

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Given <m>f(x)=7x^{\frac{1}{2}}-x+e</m> and <m>g(x)=\ln(x)-\frac{1}{x}</m>, find the following;
    </p>
</introduction>

  <task workspace="1in">
      <p><m>f'(x)</m></p>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
      <p><m>g'(x)</m></p>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
      <p><m>h'(x)</m> where <m>h(x)=f(x)+g(x)</m></p>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
      <p><m>h'(1)</m></p>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
      <p><m>j'(x)</m> where <m>j(x)=f(x)\cdot g(x)</m></p>
  </task>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="7">

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Use
      <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{3x^{-3}}+\frac{x^2}{2}-4x</m>
      to answer the questions below. Leave all answers in exact form (i.e., fractions, logarithmic, etc.).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find <m>f'(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the slope of the line tangent to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the values of <m>x</m> where the slope of the line tangent to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> is <m>2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equation of the line tangent to the graph of <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=-4</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="8">

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Given
      <m>h(x)=\frac{1}{3}x^3+6\ln(x)</m>
      and the graph of <m>f(x)</m> below, find
      <m>g'(-6)</m>
      given
      <m>g(x)=h(x)+f(x)</m>.


    <image source="MATH142_HOGU_Session4_Fig1.png" width="60%">
    <shortdescription>Graph of a function plotted on the coordinate axes. Refer to image description for more detail.</shortdescription>
    <description>
      <p>
        Graph of a linear, piece-wise defined function <m>f(x)</m> plotted on the coordinate axes. Starting from the left, the function is a constant line to the point <m>(-7, 5)</m>.
        The function decreases to the point <m>(-5,1)</m> before then increasing to the point <m>(-2,4)</m>. 
        The function is then a constant line from the point <m>(-2, 4)</m> to the point until it leave the graph on the right.
      </p>
    </description>
    </image>
    </p>
  </introduction>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="9">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Match the correct formula to the correct sentence.
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths ="65% 35%" valign ="middle">
    <ol marker="(a)">
      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>Exact</em> cost of making the <m>n^{th}</m> item is <fillin characters="15" />.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>Exact</em> revenue of making the <m>n^{th}</m> item is <fillin characters="15" />.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>Exact</em> profit of making the <m>n^{th}</m> item is <fillin characters="15" />.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>Approximate</em> cost of making the <m>n^{th}</m> item is <fillin characters="15" />.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>Approximate</em> revenue of making the <m>n^{th}</m> item is <fillin characters="15" />.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <em>Approximate</em> profit of making the <m>n^{th}</m> item is <fillin characters="15" />.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <ul>
      <li><m>P'(n-1)</m></li>
      <li><m>P(n)-P(n-1)</m></li>
      <li><m>C'(n-1)</m></li>
      <li><m>C(n)-C(n-1)</m></li>
      <li><m>R'(n-1)</m></li>
      <li><m>R(n)-R(n-1)</m></li>
    </ul>
    </sidebyside>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="10">

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The price-demand function <m>p(x)</m> and cost function <m>C(x)</m> for the production of electric saws are given by
      <m>p(x)=750-0.05x^2</m>
      and
      <m>C(x)=5800+35x</m>,
      where <m>x</m> is the number of electric saws that are sold at a price of <m>p(x)</m> dollars per saw, and <m>C(x)</m> is the cost, in dollars, of producing <m>x</m> electric saws.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the revenue and profit functions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hint: <m>R(x)=x\cdot p(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the approximate profit of making and selling the <m>65^{th}</m> saw. Round to the nearest cent if necessary.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the exact profit of making and selling the <m>65^{th}</m> saw. Round to the nearest cent if necessary.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="11">
    <statement>
        <p>
           Find
           <m>
               \D f'(x)
           </m>
           given
           <m>
               \D f(x)=4x^{2}(3x^{2/3}+5x)
           </m>
           .
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="12">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find
      <m>\frac{d}{dx}\bigg( 6x^2 \cdot 3^x + \frac{3}{x^{-2}} \bigg)</m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="13">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the equation of the line tangent to the graph of
      <m>g(x)=\ln(x)\sqrt{x}</m>
      at <m>x=1</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="14">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Determine if the work below is correct. If it is not correct, state why and work the problem correctly.
    </p>

    <p>
      Given <m>f(x)=\frac{\sqrt{x}}{2^x}</m>,
      <m>
        f'(x)=\frac{\frac{d}{dx}\bigg(\sqrt{x}\bigg)}{\frac{d}{dx}\bigg(2^x\bigg)}
        =
        \frac{\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2}}{2^x\ln 2}
      </m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in" number="15">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Given
      <m>g(x)=\frac{\sqrt[3]{x^2}+3}{e^x-4x^3}</m>,
      find <m>g'(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
        <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
  <exercise workspace="1in" number="1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        At which <m>x</m>-value(s) does <m>f(x) = x^{2} +6x-7</m> have a horizontal tangent line?
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>x = -3</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x = -7</m> and <m>x=1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x = 7</m> and <m>x = -1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x = 6</m></p></li>
            <li><p>Graph of <m>f</m> has no horizontal tangent lines</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in" number="2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The derivative of <m>f(x) = \frac{13x^{2}}{e^{x}+1}</m> is
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{13x^{2}e^{x}-26x(e^{x}+1)}{(e^{x}+1)^{2}}</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{13x^{2}-e^{x}-1}{e^{x}+1}</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{26x(e^{x}+1)-13x^{2}e^{x}}{e^{x}+1}</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>f'(x) = \frac{26x(e^{x}+1)-13x^{2}e^{x}}{(e^{x}+1)^{2}}</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in" number="3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find <m>h'(2)</m> given that <m>f(2) = -2</m>, <m>f'(2) = 5</m>, and <m>h(x) = (3x^{2})\cdot f(x)</m>
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><p><m>12</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>60</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>-24</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>36</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>-60</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</paragraphs>

  </worksheet>

